---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI installation" activeNav="/cli/installation">
  <h1>Installation</h1>
  <p>
    <code>appmctl</code> is one Bash script. Installing it means putting that script on
    <code>PATH</code>; there is no package to build and no runtime to provision.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Required for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Bash 3.2+</td><td>All commands. macOS system Bash is supported.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>curl</code></td><td>Every API request</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>jq</code></td><td><code>--json</code>, formatted output, and <code>stream</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mktemp</code>, <code>tee</code>, <code>dd</code></td><td><code>stream</code> and secure temporary files</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>sha256sum</code> or <code>shasum</code></td><td>Checksum verification during install</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Without <code>jq</code> the CLI still performs requests, but any command that formats a
    response fails with <code>jq is required for this operation</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>The installer requires a checksum</h2>
  <p>
    <strong><code>APPMCTL_SHA256</code> is mandatory.</strong> The installer refuses to run
    without it, because piping a remote script into <code>bash</code> with no verification
    is exactly how a compromised upstream reaches your runners:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="text" code={`appmctl installer: APPMCTL_SHA256 is required (set APPMCTL_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED=1 only for an explicitly trusted development source)`} />
  <p>
    So a real install is a two-step operation: read the checksum for the revision you
    intend to install, then install that exact revision.
  </p>

  <h3>Install a pinned revision</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`APPMCTL_REF='FULL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA'
BASE="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/$&#123;APPMCTL_REF&#125;"

# 1. Compute the checksum of the executable at that revision.
EXPECTED=$(curl -fsSL "$BASE/libs/cli/bin/appmctl" | shasum -a 256 | awk '{print $1}')
echo "$EXPECTED"

# 2. Install it, with the checksum enforced.
curl -fsSL "$BASE/libs/cli/install.sh" | \\
  APPMCTL_REF="$APPMCTL_REF" \\
  APPMCTL_SHA256="$EXPECTED" \\
  bash

export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
appmctl --version`} />
  <p>
    In a pipeline, record the expected checksum in the repository or in a CI variable rather
    than recomputing it — recomputing from the same URL you are about to install proves the
    download was not corrupted in transit, but it cannot tell you the upstream file changed.
    A stored value does.
  </p>

  <h3>Install to a specific directory</h3>
  <p>
    CI runners usually want the executable inside the workspace or a temp directory that is
    cleaned between jobs:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`curl -fsSL "$BASE/libs/cli/install.sh" | \\
  APPMCTL_REF="$APPMCTL_REF" \\
  APPMCTL_SHA256="$EXPECTED" \\
  APPMCTL_INSTALL_DIR="$PWD/.appmctl-bin" \\
  bash

export PATH="$PWD/.appmctl-bin:$PATH"`} />

  <h3>Unverified install, local development only</h3>
  <p>
    <code>APPMCTL_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED=1</code> skips the checksum requirement. Use it when you
    are installing from a source you already control, never in CI:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`curl -fsSL \\
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/libs/cli/install.sh | \\
  APPMCTL_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED=1 bash`} />

  <h2>Installer variables</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_SHA256</code></td><td>—</td><td>Expected SHA-256, 64 hex characters. Required unless explicitly waived.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td>Set to <code>1</code> to install without a checksum</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_INSTALL_DIR</code></td><td><code>$HOME/.local/bin</code></td><td>Target directory; created if missing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_REF</code></td><td><code>main</code></td><td>Git ref to download from</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_REPOSITORY</code></td><td><code>karerckor/app-manager</code></td><td>Source repository</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APPMCTL_DOWNLOAD_URL</code></td><td>derived</td><td>Full URL to the executable, overriding ref and repository</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The download is staged into a mode-<code>0600</code> temporary file inside the install
    directory and only moved into place after the checksum matches, so a failed or tampered
    download never lands on <code>PATH</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Run from a checkout</h2>
  <p>The committed executable runs as-is, so a clone needs no build step:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`libs/cli/bin/appmctl --help`} />
  <p>
    If you change anything under <code>libs/cli/lib/</code>, rebuild the single-file
    executable — the modules are bundled into <code>bin/appmctl</code>, and the tests run
    against the bundle, not the sources:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`bash libs/cli/build.sh > libs/cli/bin/appmctl
chmod +x libs/cli/bin/appmctl

for t in libs/cli/tests/test_*.sh; do bash "$t"; done`} />

  <h2>Verify the install</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --version      # appmctl 0.3.0
appmctl --help         # resource list and global options
appmctl builds --help  # actions for one resource`} />
  <p>
    Next: <a href="/cli/configuration">Configuration</a> covers the environment
    variables and the API key scopes each command needs.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
